        "A study plan for this town has to survive two local realities that no national calendar mentions. The first is the sugarcane crushing season, which runs roughly from November into March. Lorries and tractor trailers queue for the co-operative factories, the highway through town slows, harvesting households work long days, and labour families who migrate for cutting take children with them. Any plan that assumes a calm December here has not been written by somebody who lives here.",
        "The second is the monsoon. Rain arrives in June off the Western Ghats and runs through September, the Hiranyakeshi fills, and in bad years the Krishna and Ghataprabha basin floods have closed schools across the Chikodi and Hukkeri belt for days at a stretch. Online lessons hold up better than travel in those weeks, with one honest exception: when the power and the mobile network go together, nothing happens, and we reschedule without charging for the missed hour.",
        "Against that sit the fixed examination dates. The IB examines in May and in November, and IGCSE runs the May and June series and the October and November series, with January available on some Edexcel subjects. Reading those against the local year, the useful insight is that July to September is the quiet window for building topic coverage and drafting internal assessments, even though the weather is at its worst, because nothing is due and the household is not yet in crushing season.",
        "Festivals need to be planned for rather than apologised for. Ugadi opens the year, Kara Hunnime and Nagara Panchami fall before and during the sowing and rain months, Ganesh Chaturthi is substantial in this border belt, Dasara and Deepavali take out a long autumn stretch, and the Yellamma jatre at Saundatti draws families from across the district in the cold months. We ask for the dates that matter to your household at the start and schedule around them instead of discovering them one Tuesday evening.",
